In Terraform, state file is the heart of the terraform. Or you can consider it as a brain of the terraform. 
So whenever Terraform creates a resource on cloud provider like AWS, Terraform stores this information on to the state file 
to understand what resources it has created. So in future, if it has to update the resource that it created, it will just go back to the state file.
Understand what is the current state of the resource And then it will understand what needs to be updated.

So what exactly Terraform does every time it creates a resource, it can be as simple as an EC2 instance. It goes to this file called as state file.
And within this state file 1. it stores the resource ID of the EC2 instance. 
2. It stores the metadata of the EC2 instance.
3. It stores the IP address of the instance that it created, and it also stores the last known state of the EC2 instance.
So next time, if it has to update very simple, it will see if the resource ID exists.

Now when user asks to update something, it will see what is the difference between the last known state and what is the user asking to update.
If user is asking me to update a small change on the EC2 instance. So I'll just make an API call to AWS to update that small change on the EC2 instance.
So it is capable of doing all these only because of the state file.

Imagine if state file does not exist. So you go to Terraform. You ask Terraform to create an EC2 instance. Okay, Terraform makes an API call to AWS.
It creates the EC2 instance. After one month you know you wrote a CL file for EC2 instance creation. 
After one month you updated this Excel file with a particular key value pair. That's it. Now Terraform looks at this Excel file.
It does not know that the EC2 instance already exists on AWS. It will just see the Excel file.

Somebody provided me this information and they ran Terraform apply. So I'm going to make an API call to AWS to create this instance.
Again because it does not know that the instance exists. So again it makes an API call with AWS. It might fail or it might create a new EC2 instance.
This is avoided only because of the state file. That's why state file is called as brain of Terraform.
Whenever you run Terraform plan or whenever you run Terraform apply. You must have noticed Terraform gives you a prompt saying that these are the additions 
that I am going to make. And these are the deletions that I am going to make, or these are the modifications that I am going to make.

In Short: 

In Terraform state, file stores the information of the resources that are created by Terraform. It also stores all the relevant information, 
such as the metadata of the resources, last known information of the resources. 
So whenever you run Terraform plan or Terraform apply, Terraform looks at the state file and understands the current state of the resources.
Only because of the state file, Terraform can execute the actions.
